# DS2102 in a tabular `let` (reported once, where it is defined) and on the right of `join` and `union`.
mode: check
schema: t(x: long, y: real, s: string, ts: datetime, b: bool)
---- query
let ones = from t | where 1 == 1;
from ones | join (from t | where false) on x | union ones, (from t | where true)
---- expected
warning[DS2102]: `where` condition is always true
  --> query:1:27
  |
1 | let ones = from t | where 1 == 1;
  |                           ^^^^^^ true for every row, so `where` keeps them all
  |
  = help: if this is on purpose, remove the `where`: it has no effect

info[DS1007]: `join` renamed 4 columns of the right table: `y` to `y1`, `s` to `s1`, `ts` to `ts1`, `b` to `b1`
  --> query:2:18
  |
2 | from ones | join (from t | where false) on x | union ones, (from t | where true)
  |                  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ this table has columns with the same names as the left side
  |
  = help: to choose the names yourself, rename them on the right: `join (from t | where false | project-rename right_y = y, right_s = s, right_ts = ts, right_b = b)`
  = note: a column named in `on` is not renamed: the two sides make one column (only `$left.a == $right.b` keeps both)

warning[DS2102]: `where` condition is always false
  --> query:2:34
  |
2 | from ones | join (from t | where false) on x | union ones, (from t | where true)
  |                                  ^^^^^ false for every row, so the result is empty
  |
  = help: to get the columns with no rows, use `take 0`

warning[DS2102]: `where` condition is always true
  --> query:2:76
  |
2 | from ones | join (from t | where false) on x | union ones, (from t | where true)
  |                                                                            ^^^^ true for every row, so `where` keeps them all
  |
  = help: if this is on purpose, remove the `where`: it has no effect
